            <chunk name="Object" index="11">
              <items count="3">
                <item name="GUID" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">3f6b2e1d-8a47-4c95-b1e3-7d20c5a9e614</item>
                <item name="Lib" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">82b21a65-da8f-433c-93fb-f9814609e1d6</item>
                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Wind Climate</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="4">
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Long-term directional wind statistics for pedestrian comfort: for every direction sector (36 × 10° by default) and season (year + Dec-Feb, Mar-May, Jun-Aug, Sep-Nov, named for the hemisphere), the sector probability, the calm share and a Weibull c, k fitted by maximum likelihood to the non-calm hours of a MULTI-YEAR record — ERA5 via Open-Meteo (any coordinate, no key), the nearest NOAA ISD or GHCNh station, local EPW files or a CSV — or the City of London Annex A table. A typical year cannot carry a safety criterion: Lawson's 15 m/s for 0.022 % of the year is about two hours, and a TMY holds two hours of one composite year. Wire the Wind Climate output into Pedestrian Wind Comfort (with VAF's Speed Ratios) to classify from P(U &gt; u) = Σ p·exp(−(u/(r·c))^k); the report says whether the record is long enough to measure the safety tail. Downloads run off the canvas and are cached per year in WeatherCache/WindRecords inside the Eddy3D folder of your local app data.Sources: ERA5 (Hersbach et al. 2020, Copernicus C3S) via Open-Meteo; NOAA NCEI Integrated Surface Database (ISD-Lite) and its successor GHCNh; City of London Wind Microclimate Guidelines (2019), Annex A.

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.21.0.827

Version 1.21.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">1181f22f-4e6b-4ef3-b9d7-51f276091a18</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Wind Climate</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Wind Climate</item>
                  </items>
                  <chunks count="24">
                    <chunk name="Attributes">
                      <items count="2">
                        <item name="Bounds" type_name="gh_drawing_rectanglef" type_code="35">
                          <X>929</X>
                          <Y>2090</Y>
                          <W>339</W>
                          <H>264</H>
                        </item>
                        <item name="Pivot" type_name="gh_drawing_pointf" type_code="31">
                          <X>1037</X>
                          <Y>2222</Y>
                        </item>
                      </items>
                    </chunk>
                    <chunk name="param_input" index="0">
                      <items count="9">
                        <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Where the wind comes from. ERA5 (Open-Meteo): hourly 10 m reanalysis for the 0.25° cell at Latitude/Longitude, open-terrain exposure. ISD station (NOAA): the nearest station within 100 km covering half the years (or the one named in Station); NOAA stopped updating ISD-Lite in August 2025. GHCNh station (NOAA): its successor, current to the day before, for 2000 onward — the nearest station within 100 km that measures wind (screened on the last year), or the one named in Station; 12-34 MB a station-year, read once and kept as a small file. EPW files / CSV file: the paths in Files. City of London Annex A: the guideline's published table (c at 120 m, terrain-corrected for the City — not valid elsewhere). Global Wind Atlas (.lib file): a generalized wind climate you downloaded from globalwindatlas.info, path in Files — the class nearest Source Terrain's roughness length, at Reference Height (between the file's heights, linear in ln z; outside them it refuses). It is the wind over FLAT terrain of that roughness, not the local wind, and it has no seasons.</item>
